In this exercise, you will practice the Decorator design pattern by building a simple text formatting system for a console-based “editor.”
Use decorators to dynamically add behavior (bold, italic, underline, red color) to a plain text object.

This exercise mirrors the Beverage + Condiments example you saw in class, but with text instead of coffee drinks.

Tasks:

1. Write the Abstract Decorator -> TextDecorator
Write code in the TextDecorator class that implements Text.
It should store an instance variable Text decoratedText.

Its render() method should just delegate to decoratedText.render().
This step is essential to the pattern: a decorator is-a Text, but also has-a Text!

2. Implement Concrete Decorators
Each class should extend TextDecorator and wrap the output of super.render() with ANSI codes:

BoldDecorator → AnsiCodes.BOLD … AnsiCodes.RESET (see Bold Decorator).

Implement other decorators:
ItalicDecorator
UnderlineDecorator
RedDecorator

3. Fill in code in the DecoratorDemo class with a main method that:

Creates a PlainText:
Text text = new PlainText("Hello, world!");
Wraps it in different decorators (BoldDecorator, ItalicDecorator, UnderlineDecorator, RedDecorator).
Prints the results to the console.
